Case Study: Phishing Attack on College Students

Phishing is a type of cybercrime where attackers trick individuals into revealing sensitive information such as passwords, bank details,
or OTPs by pretending to be a trustworthy entity. This often happens through emails, messages, or fake websites.

A common scenario involves students receiving an email that appears to be from their university or a well-known company.
The message may claim that their account will be suspended unless they verify their details. The email includes a link that leads
to a fake website designed to look legitimate. When the student enters their login credentials or financial details, the attacker captures this information.

Typically, students and young adults are targeted because they frequently use online services and may not always verify the authenticity of messages. 
Attackers also exploit urgency and fear to push victims into acting quickly without thinking.

The consequences can be serious. Victims may lose access to their accounts, face financial loss, or have their personal data misused for identity theft.
In some cases, compromised accounts are further used to target others.

This highlights the importance of awareness, verifying sources, and never sharing sensitive information through untrusted links or messages.
